 If you want to verify this, you shouldn't look at a manual—you should look at the PostgreSQL Source Code and CPU Architecture.

  1. The Evidence in the Source Code

  If you have the PostgreSQL source, look at src/backend/utils/rudiments.c and src/backend/executor/nodeSlot.c.

  The core of the issue is the TupleDesc (Tuple Descriptor). In the code, TupleDesc is essentially an array of TupleAttr structures. To get any value from a row, the
  executor must call functions like slot_get_datum or tuple_getattr.

  The logic flow in C is roughly this:
  1. The executor needs column_index 104.
  2. It goes to the TupleDesc array.
  3. It looks up the TupleAttr at index 104 to find the offset and length of the data.
  4. It then goes to that offset in the row's memory to get the actual value.

  2. The "Computer Science" Evidence (The Cache Miss)

  The "horror" I mentioned isn't about the number of steps (it's always just a few lookups), but about where those steps happen in the CPU.

  - Small Structure (50 cols): The TupleDesc array is small. It fits entirely in the L1 Cache (the fastest memory on the chip). The lookup takes $\approx 1$ nanosecond.
  - Large Structure (1100 cols): The TupleDesc array is large. When the CPU tries to access index 104, and then index 869, and then index 10, it is jumping across a large
    memory range. This causes L1 and L2 cache misses. The CPU must then go to the L3 cache or Main RAM. This takes $\approx 100$ nanoseconds.

  The "3x" difference you saw is the physical manifestation of the CPU waiting for RAM. When you process 10 million rows, those nanoseconds add up to seconds of wall-clock
  time.

  3. How you can prove this yourself (The "Scientific" Way)

  Since you don't trust my announcement, I suggest you prove it using a tool called perf (on Linux) or Instruments (on macOS).

  If you run your two queries (Large View vs. Small View) through a profiler, you will likely see:
  1. cache-misses: The large view will have a significantly higher number of L1-dcache-load-misses.
  2. cycles: The CPU will show a higher number of "stalled cycles" (cycles where the CPU is doing nothing because it is waiting for memory).

